## Context

SPEC.md §5.2.10, landed in PR #418, requires a minimum question count per
MUST cell of the lane matrix and deliberately states no number. The pass that
wrote it judged, and said so, that inventing one would be a floor authored to
clear rather than a floor derived from anything.

The reason the rule exists: a cell with one or two questions cannot decide
anything, and a floor of "100 % of pinned answers in the first ten" over a
two-question cell is a coin flip on a single authoring accident. The review
panel of 2026-09-06 raised it twice, from the evaluation seat and the red
team, as the gap between a gate and a benchmark.

The number cannot be set from the armchair. It follows from what the bank
actually holds once the authoring lanes finish (ticket 0722): the per-cell
counts, and how unevenly they fall across the lane pairs R7 and R29 bind.

## Actions

1. When the bank is complete, count the questions per MUST cell of the lane
   matrix, per stratum and per mode, and report the distribution.
2. Propose a minimum from that distribution, with the reasoning: what a cell
   at that count can and cannot distinguish.
3. The author sets the number; record it in `DECISIONS.md` and write it into
   SPEC.md §5.2.10.
4. State what happens to a MUST cell below the minimum: per R7's own
   set-aside mechanism it reports not-evaluated with a stated reason, rather
   than passing or failing on too few questions.

## Blocked-by
Waits on ticket 0722's bank being complete enough to count.
Satisfied 2026-09-07: the bank landed on main at PR #409 (`493ea13`),
281 questions under `bench/fixtures/questions/`, schema `menagerie-bank/v3`.

## The measured distribution (action 1)

Counted by `bench/bank_cells.py` over the committed bank, cross-checked
against the committed golden-gate report; artifact at
`bench/results/0733-bank-cells/cells.json`, regenerated by

    python3 bench/bank_cells.py --minimum 12 --sweep 2 30 \
        --output bench/results/0733-bank-cells/cells.json

Every number in this ticket comes out of that command. The floor sweep below
is `floor_sweep` in the artifact, not arithmetic done by hand beside it: a
first draft of that table extrapolated two of its rows and got both wrong.

The MUST cells are R7's first tier squared: R7 makes English, French and
Vietnamese normative, and R29 binds all six ordered pairs over that tier.
Nine cells. Every other lane in the bank rides R7's second tier, where a
set-aside is allowed if stated, and is counted apart.

| cell | bank | retrieval | expected-miss | core | reserve | gate scored |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| en->en | 94 | 56 | 38 | 53 | 3 | 56 |
| en->fr | 4 | 4 | 0 | 4 | 0 | 4 |
| en->vi | 10 | 8 | 2 | 8 | 0 | 8 |
| fr->en | 12 | 10 | 2 | 10 | 0 | 10 |
| fr->fr | 32 | 19 | 13 | 19 | 0 | 19 |
| fr->vi | 7 | 7 | 0 | 7 | 0 | 7 |
| vi->en | 13 | 11 | 2 | 11 | 0 | 11 |
| vi->fr | 2 | 2 | 0 | 2 | 0 | 2 |
| vi->vi | 45 | 33 | 12 | 29 | 4 | 33 |
| **MUST total** | **219** | **150** | **69** | 143 | 7 | **150** |
| non-MUST (13 second-tier lanes) | 62 | 45 | 17 | | | 45 |
| **bank total** | **281** | **195** | **86** | | | **195** |

**How uneven.** Over the nine MUST cells the bank counts run 2 to 94, a factor
of 47, with a Gini of 0,55 against a flat matrix's 0. On the retrieval counts —
the population a rate is computed over — they run 2 to 56, a factor of 28, Gini
0,49, median 10 against a flat mean of 16,7. Three cells (`en->en`, `vi->vi`,
`fr->fr`) hold 72 % of the MUST retrieval questions; the six cross-lingual
cells R29 binds hold the remaining 42 between them, and one of them holds two.

**Two counts, and which one the minimum should bind.** The bank count includes
expected-miss records, which §5.2.10 keeps deliberately and never deletes; they
are gated on the mechanism that hides the answer, not on retrieval, so they
carry no information about whether the lane retrieves. The retrieval count is
the population R34's rate is computed over. **The minimum binds the retrieval
count.** The bank count is reported beside it because it is the number an
authoring lane can move, and because a cell rich in bank questions and poor in
reachable ones (`en->en`: 94 against 56, `fr->fr`: 32 against 19) would read as
healthy on the wrong column.

**Cross-check against the gate.** `bench/results/golden/report.json` records
per-lane scored counts of en->en 56, fr->fr 19, vi->vi 33, en->fr 4, en->vi 8,
fr->en 10, fr->vi 7, vi->en 11, vi->fr 2 — 150 over the MUST cells, 195 over
the whole bank. The bank's own retrieval counts reproduce every one of those
nine numbers exactly, and 195 = 281 − 86 expected-miss. The two quantities are
not the same thing and the difference is fully accounted: the gate's 195 is
what it scored in *this replay*, which is 281 questions minus 86 expected-miss
records; the 562 not-run pairs are the same 281 questions asked again in the
two modes the offline replay does not run. Where the two could diverge and
today do not: a question whose reply is missing, whose `run` block is absent,
or whose declared mode is not the mode run, would be scored by neither and
counted by the bank. The cross-check is therefore a real check rather than a
tautology, and `tests/test_bank_cells.py` holds a case where it comes out
*disagreeing*, so its silence means something.

**Per mode, the axis is currently degenerate, and this matters for the floor.**
All 281 questions declare `mode: any`. No MUST cell has a per-mode split to
count, so a minimum written against the (cell × declared mode) product would
put every cell of the matrix at zero and print the whole thing not-measured.
The report's `by_mode` groups by the mode the *reply* was produced in, not by
the question's declared mode: 195 lexical, 0 semantic, 0 hybrid, the last two
carrying §5.2.8's not-run reason (embeddings are off in the offline replay).
**Recommendation: the minimum binds the cell, once, and not the cell × mode
product.** A question is `any` precisely because it is asked in every mode that
runs, so its contribution to the cell does not depend on the run's mode set;
and a mode that did not run is already not-run under §5.2.8, which is a
different and correctly-named state from a cell too thin to read.

## The proposed minimum (action 2 — proposal; the ruling is the author's)

**Proposed: 12 retrieval questions per MUST cell.**

**What the gate actually demands, and why that sets the arithmetic.** R34
tolerates no miss: a cell passes only when every one of its questions returns
its answer within the first ten. A cell is therefore a sequence of n Bernoulli
trials read under an all-must-pass rule, and two questions follow — what a
clean cell certifies, and what a dirty one lets a reader distinguish.

**What a clean cell of size n certifies.** If a lane's true per-question miss
rate is q, a clean cell of n questions is evidence that q is small, and the
one-sided 95 % upper bound is 1 − 0,05^(1/n):

| n | 95 % upper bound on the lane's miss rate | power against a 25 % regression | power against a 10 % regression | resolution of the printed rate |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 2 | 77,6 % | 43,8 % | 19,0 % | 50,0 pts |
| 4 | 52,7 % | 68,4 % | 34,4 % | 25,0 pts |
| 7 | 34,8 % | 86,7 % | 52,2 % | 14,3 pts |
| 10 | 25,9 % | 94,4 % | 65,1 % | 10,0 pts |
| **12** | **22,1 %** | **96,8 %** | **71,8 %** | **8,3 pts** |
| 15 | 18,1 % | 98,7 % | 79,4 % | 6,7 pts |
| 20 | 13,9 % | 99,7 % | 87,8 % | 5,0 pts |
| 30 | 9,5 % | 100,0 % | 95,8 % | 3,3 pts |

`vi->fr`'s two questions, passing clean, certify only that the lane misses
fewer than four queries in five. That is not a weak claim, it is no claim: R7
and R29 would be gated by a statement almost any broken system satisfies. At
twelve the same clean pass certifies fewer than one in four, and catches a
regression costing the lane a quarter of its questions 97 times in 100.

**What a dirty cell of size n lets a reader distinguish — the ticket's own
failure, quantified.** Under an all-must-pass rule a *single* bad question
turns any cell red at any n, so the floor buys no robustness against an
authoring accident; nothing does, and raising n makes a cell strictly more
likely to catch one. What n buys is that the rate printed beside the verdict
becomes readable. §5.2.10 requires the count beside the rate for exactly this.
At n = 2 the rate takes three values — 0, 50, 100 % — and one authoring
accident and a half-collapsed lane are the *same printed number*: the reviewer
cannot tell them apart, which is the coin flip the ticket names. At n = 12 an
accident prints 11/12 = 92 % and a lane that lost a quarter of its retrieval
prints 9/12 = 75 %, two resolution steps apart with a distinct value between
them. Two steps needs three failures against one, so it needs n ≥ 12 exactly;
below that the two readings are adjacent or identical.

**The bound gives a band, not a point, and this is said plainly.** Requiring
the clean-pass bound below one in four takes n ≥ 11; below one in five it takes
n ≥ 14. Anything in 11–14 is defensible on the statistics alone and the
arithmetic does not single out one of them. What is not defensible is anything
at or below 7, where a clean pass certifies a miss rate above one in three, or
at or below 4, where a clean cell is compatible with the lane failing half its
queries.

**What picks 12 out of that band is the shape of this bank, and it is
measured.** Which MUST cells a floor sets aside, and what closing them costs in
further retrieval questions:

| floor | 95 % bound | cells set aside | questions to author |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 8 | 31,2 % | 3 | 11 |
| 10 | 25,9 % | 4 | 19 |
| 11 | 23,8 % | 5 | 24 |
| **12** | **22,1 %** | **6** | **30** |
| 15 | 18,1 % | 6 | 48 |
| 19 | 14,6 % | 6 | 72 |
| 20 | 13,9 % | 7 | 79 |

Twelve is the **lower edge of a plateau**. From 12 through 19 the set-aside set
is identical — exactly R29's six cross-lingual cells, and no monolingual one —
because the next cell above is `fr->fr` at 19. Anywhere on that plateau the
floor gates the same cells and says the same thing about the same promises;
raising it buys a tighter bound at strictly more authoring and no change in
coverage, 72 questions at 19 against 30 at 12. Below 12 the floor starts
sparing cross-lingual cells by a one-question margin — 11 spares `vi->en`,
which holds exactly 11 — and a line drawn where one authored question decides a
lane's gating is arbitrary in the way a floor should not be. So: the cheapest
point of the plateau whose coverage the author would actually want.

**Why the floor is uniform across the nine cells.** The six cross-lingual cells
are the expensive ones to author — each needs a document pair, not a document —
and a lower floor for them is the obvious accommodation. It is also precisely
the parking §5.2.10 forbids: "so that the hard questions cannot be parked where
nothing gates". R29's promise is not a weaker promise than R7's; the cells that
are hardest to fill are the ones whose emptiness the floor exists to make
visible. One number, nine cells.

**How many MUST cells 12 sets aside: six of nine.** `en->fr` (4), `en->vi` (8),
`fr->en` (10), `fr->vi` (7), `vi->en` (11), `vi->fr` (2) all fall short;
`en->en` (56), `fr->fr` (19) and `vi->vi` (33) clear it. Every set-aside cell
is one of R29's six cross-lingual pairs, and every cleared cell is one of R7's
three monolingual lanes — the floor's first act is to say out loud that the
cross-lingual promise is currently gated by nothing. Closing the six costs 30
further retrieval questions: 8 + 4 + 2 + 5 + 10 + 1, per cell in that order.
That consequence is information, not an argument for a lower number; a floor
chosen so that today's bank clears it would be the floor §5.2.10 refused to
invent.

**Defensible on both readings of R34, and not tuned to today's zero.** The
floor binds a *count*, so no score enters its derivation. For the record of
what it is not tuned to: the golden gate today reads OFFICIAL 0/195 because no
hit carries a page (ticket 0734) against ACCOMMODATING 51/195, and per MUST
cell the accommodating reading is 45/150 — en->en 29/56, fr->fr 10/19,
vi->vi 5/33, vi->en 1/11, and 0 in the five other cross-lingual cells, which
cannot be otherwise while the replay runs lexical-only. None of those numbers
moved the proposal, and none of them would move it if the official score rose
to the 85/195 ceiling ticket 0734 measured.

## The below-minimum behaviour (action 4)

Stated in SPEC.md §5.2.10, paragraph "The total is soft; the cells are not",
and verified against R7's text rather than inferred:

- A MUST cell holding **fewer questions than its minimum** prints
  **not-evaluated**, with its count beside it, rather than a rate computed on
  too few questions. It is neither a pass nor a failure.
- A MUST cell holding **none** prints **not-measured**.
- Both print their count beside their rate, and R34's non-vacuity rides on
  those minima and on nothing else.

The mechanism §5.2.10 borrows is R7's: "Setting a second-tier language aside is
allowed and MUST be stated, which is what separates a tier from a wish"
(SPEC.md R7), which R29 extends to second-tier pairs. What is borrowed is the
*form* — a set-aside is allowed only when it is stated, and the statement names
the reason. The reason here is mechanical and always the same shape: *this cell
holds n questions against a minimum of m*.

One distinction the author may want SPEC to make explicit, recorded here rather
than edited in: R7's own clause sets aside a **promise** — a second-tier
language may be left unsupported if that is stated. §5.2.10's clause sets aside
a **reading** — the MUST promise stands in full, and what is withheld is a
verdict the evidence cannot support. A cell printing not-evaluated is not a
lane that has been downgraded; it is a lane nobody has yet gated. On the
current bank that difference is load-bearing for six of the nine cells, so it
is worth a sentence somewhere rather than being left to the reader.

## Exit criteria
- [x] the bank's per-cell counts measured and reported
- [ ] a minimum proposed with its reasoning, and ruled by the author
      — proposed 2026-09-07 (12 retrieval questions per MUST cell, above);
      the ruling is the author's and this box stays open until he makes it
- [ ] the number written into SPEC.md §5.2.10 and recorded in DECISIONS.md
      — the author's, and deliberately untouched here
- [x] the below-minimum behaviour stated, as not-evaluated with a reason
